#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096311 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "210f48cc-8812-564d-9dae-5a8a42165ef9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14005" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${x + 2y =-4; x - \\frac{y}{6} = \\frac{5}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6$.\n\n$$\n{x + 2y =-4; x - \\frac{y}{6} = \\frac{5}{2} \\mid \\mid \\cdot 6\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= -4 \\\\\nx &= -4 - 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $x=-4-2y$ into the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - y &= 15 \\\\\n6(-4 - 2y) - y &= 15 \\\\\n- 24 - 12y - y &= 15 \\\\\n- 24 - 13y &= 15 \\\\\n- 13y &= 39 \\\\\ny &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $x$, we substitute $y=-3$ into the equation $x=-4-2y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -4 - 2y \\\\\n&=-4 - 2(-3) \\\\\n&=-4 + 6 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 2$ and $y = -3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096312 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "ff844be7-7d1e-5a1f-a4d6-1d4313f5fd2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14006" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations:\n\n${5a + \\frac{3b}{2} = 2; \\frac{4a}{3} + 2b = 0$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions, we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{5a + \\frac{3b}{2} = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 2; \\frac{4a}{3} + 2b = 0 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 3\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6b$ term that can cancel with the $6b$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{10a + 3b = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 4a + 6b = 0\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $b$-variable, and allows us to solve for $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-20a - 6b &= -8 \\\\\n4a + 6b &= 0 \\\\\n(-20a + 4a) &= (-8 + 0) \\\\\n- 16a &= -8 \\\\\na &= \\frac{-8}{-16} \\\\\na &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the equation\n\n$$\n4a+6b=0\n$$\n\nto solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4a + 6b &= 0 \\\\\n4(\\frac{1}{2}) + 6b &= 0 \\\\\n2 + 6b &= 0 \\\\\n6b &= -2 \\\\\nb &= -\\frac{2}{6} \\\\\nb &= -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\,b =-\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096317 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "fb7a4815-45e8-5288-8e96-c3741f1a2097" :question/math-academy-id "e-14732" :question/problem "${-x - 5y =-1; \\frac{1}{4}x + 3y = 2$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $4$.\n\n$$\n{-x - 5y =-1; \\frac{1}{4}x + 3y = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 4\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $-x$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $x$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable, and allows us to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 5y &= -1 \\\\\nx + 12y &= 8 \\\\\n7y &= 7 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=1$ into the equation $-x-5y=-1$ to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 5y &= -1 \\\\\n- x - 5 \\cdot 1 &= -1 \\\\\n- x - 5 &= -1 \\\\\n- x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx=-4, \\, y=1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096318 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "7f77dc95-b22e-51af-a8c2-ef60ae4f3098" :question/math-academy-id "e-14733" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n${\\frac{5}{2}x + 2y = 3; \\frac{1}{6}x - \\frac{2}{3}y =-7$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $6$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{5}{2}x + 2y = 3 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 2; \\frac{1}{6}x - \\frac{2}{3}y =-7 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 6\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $y$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 4y &= 6 \\\\\nx - 4y &= -42 \\\\\n(5x + x) &= (6 - 42) \\\\\n6x &= -36 \\\\\nx &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-6$ into the second equation to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 4y &= -42 \\\\\n- 6 - 4y &= -42 \\\\\n- 4y &= -36 \\\\\ny &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-6$ and $y=9$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d43b0e35-5a7e-5387-b7f4-707b5aa5b6ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d43b0e35-5a7e-5387-b7f4-707b5aa5b6ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252441} :answer-field/id #uuid "56f49e56-d8f9-557f-9b51-c2db7f8ba804" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252440} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c888562-a37b-5aeb-a2e3-b0df5d06c22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c888562-a37b-5aeb-a2e3-b0df5d06c22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252443} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3a0814d-7540-5f9d-bf7d-276819200557" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252442}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81946f52-f39d-57ab-a887-d387ae9bede6" :question/math-academy-id "q-215738" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2p+4q=-6 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{2}+\\frac{5q}{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $2.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2p+4q=-6 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{2}+\\frac{5q}{2}=2 & |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2p+4q=-6 \\\\ 3p+5q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $p$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p+4q & =-6 \\\\ 2p & =-4q-6 \\\\ p & =-2q-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $p=-2q-3$ into the second equation and solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p+5q & =4 \\\\ 3(-2q-3)+5q & =4 \\\\ -6q-9+5q & =4 \\\\ -q & =13 \\\\ q & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to find the value of $p,$ we substitute $q=-13$ into the equation $p=-2q-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =-2q-3 \\\\  & =-2(-13)-3 \\\\  & =26-3 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=23,\\,q=-13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86e271e2-fbd8-5c4a-aa46-e807b51319e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252446} {:answer/id #uuid "4271c9de-f6a5-5131-9a28-e2b014bf1bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3,\\,y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252447} {:answer/id #uuid "2adec1c1-fa80-5c3e-8c1a-fcb7f93bbe03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252448} {:answer/id #uuid "df689d00-8d5c-5e2f-a8c7-6288497218ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252449} {:answer/id #uuid "f0325724-fd68-59cb-b098-66d7c2163809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2,\\,y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4271c9de-f6a5-5131-9a28-e2b014bf1bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3,\\,y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252447} :answer-field/id #uuid "c49f84c3-cdd1-5252-983a-ac70cd4e2946" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b79f1980-1979-56f6-9b8c-7d297f6d53ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-215824" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{7}+\\frac{2y}{7}=1 \\\\ x-y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $7.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{7}+\\frac{2y}{7}=1 & |\\,\\times 7 \\\\ x-y=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=7 \\\\ x-y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $x$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =1 \\\\ x & =1+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $x=1+y$ into the first equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =7 \\\\ (1+y)+2y & =7 \\\\ 1+3y & =7 \\\\ 3y & =6 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $x,$ we substitute $y=2$ into the equation $x=1+y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1+y \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3,\\,y=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d28675b3-ec2b-5200-956e-5000f5aec7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252453} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd05fa3-73b8-5bf6-84e3-a1ba006e1ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d28675b3-ec2b-5200-956e-5000f5aec7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252453} :answer-field/id #uuid "df61b658-6a58-5152-81a9-1c00ceec6a21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49079722-f69c-5424-b1d3-17711195c7be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49079722-f69c-5424-b1d3-17711195c7be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252456} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0596d75-fb40-5098-87cf-f2606e19f069" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252455}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fcca4f2-b904-5b28-bf28-2efe9e3d955f" :question/math-academy-id "q-215827" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a-b=7 \\\\ \\frac{a}{5}-\\frac{2b}{5}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $5.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a-b=7 \\\\ \\frac{a}{5}-\\frac{2b}{5}=1 & |\\,\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2a-b=7 \\\\ a-2b=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $a$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-2b & =5 \\\\ a & =5+2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $a=5+2b$ into the first equation and solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-b & =7 \\\\ 2(5+2b)-b & =7 \\\\ 10+4b-b & =7 \\\\ 3b & =-3 \\\\ b & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $a,$ we substitute $b=-1$ into the equation $a=5+2b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =5+2b \\\\  & =5+2(-1) \\\\  & =5-2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=3,\\,b=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "635c3d4e-cea3-5564-bf97-114681db564d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "635c3d4e-cea3-5564-bf97-114681db564d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252459} :answer-field/id #uuid "1616e885-2265-5e87-b6b4-eb4d6fa72228" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252458} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca439ac-c3f8-5a03-991a-66dba31344f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ca439ac-c3f8-5a03-991a-66dba31344f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252461} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7320e5d-faf3-531d-a384-17aabb8d1165" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252460}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffb18301-b485-5f96-b6f9-5a55c54a6ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-215839" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2s+t=7 \\\\ \\frac{3s}{8}+\\frac{t}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$s=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,t=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $8.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2s+t=7 \\\\ \\frac{3s}{8}+\\frac{t}{2}=1 & |\\,\\times 8\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2s+t=7 \\\\ 3s+4t=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $t$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2s+t & =7 \\\\ t & =7-2s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $t=7-2s$ into the second equation and solve for $s:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3s+4t & =8 \\\\ 3s+4(7-2s) & =8 \\\\ 3s+28-8s & =8 \\\\ -5s+28 & =8 \\\\ -5s & =-20 \\\\ s & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $t,$ we substitute $s=4$ into the equation $t=7-2s,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =7-2s \\\\  & =7-2(4) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $s=4,\\,t=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098cfbd9-b1ee-516b-b2ea-5e70d08dd8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252464} {:answer/id #uuid "d74553d7-b1fa-550d-bca5-fa8671c27b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "098cfbd9-b1ee-516b-b2ea-5e70d08dd8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252464} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd45a38b-1ad9-5c8c-ab73-bffbf5c0f632" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252463} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88ada8fe-3b8f-56c4-8cdb-a9aa7cfa0550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88ada8fe-3b8f-56c4-8cdb-a9aa7cfa0550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252467} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b470abb-2feb-5634-abbc-96081f32da5c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252466}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7cfbe2ec-3385-5eae-80b7-ab6a0cfc8ffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-215849" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{2}{5}y=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 2x-\\frac{1}{2}y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $5$ and the second equation by $2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{2}{5}y=\\frac{1}{5} & |\\,\\times 5 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{1}{2}y=1 & |\\,\\times 2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y=1 \\\\ 4x-y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Solving for $y$ in the second equation we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-y & =2 \\\\ -y & =-4x+2 \\\\ y & =4x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=4x-2$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2y & =1 \\\\ 5x-2(4x-2) & =1 \\\\ 5x-8x+4 & =1 \\\\ -3x+4 & =1 \\\\ -3x & =-3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x=1$ into the equation $y=4x-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x-2 \\\\  & =4(1)-2 \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1,\\,y=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08af4282-6c1f-5ead-a2f7-3f60bf01edfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08af4282-6c1f-5ead-a2f7-3f60bf01edfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252470} :answer-field/id #uuid "a61997b0-5df8-5fa7-8284-936c954aa008" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252469} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05216356-4221-5c52-b37c-391394706db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05216356-4221-5c52-b37c-391394706db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252472} :answer-field/id #uuid "915186b8-6215-5ebb-935e-9dee0123a0bf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ca740a3-72d4-5cec-bd44-d4347f714dcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-215856" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}y=2 \\\\ x+\\frac{4}{7}y=-\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}y=2 & |\\times 3 \\\\ x+\\frac{4}{7}y=-\\frac{3}{7} & |\\times 7\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x+7y=6 \\\\ 7x+4y=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the first equation can easily be solved for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7y & =6 \\\\ x & =6-7y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=6-7y$ into the second equation and then solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+4y & =-3 \\\\ 7(6-7y)+4y & =-3 \\\\ 42-49y+4y & =-3 \\\\ -45y & =-45 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $x,$ we substitute $y=1$ into the equation $x=6-7y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6-7y\\text{ } \\\\  & =6-7(1) \\\\  & =6-7 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2701b96-8c48-5e59-8e22-0700a85feb13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252475} {:answer/id #uuid "38031efc-c416-5439-8c0b-7c1d67f23c99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2701b96-8c48-5e59-8e22-0700a85feb13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252475} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2fa9c7-cdcf-5e1d-bde2-0964c62f4ef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5c44155-0f2e-59ba-be7c-8a7c1f96b015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5c44155-0f2e-59ba-be7c-8a7c1f96b015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252478} :answer-field/id #uuid "08f3bfb3-9a73-59af-96b0-e84c0a9b7362" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252477}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffd5bd9a-1589-5f50-ab84-6ad9ec3a97cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-216037" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}a+2b=5 \\\\ -3a+8b=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $4.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}a+2b=5 & |\\,\\times 4 \\\\ -3a+8b=-4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3a+8b=20 \\\\ -3a+8b=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $3a$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-3a$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $a$-variable, and allows us to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3a}+8b & =20 \\\\ -\\cancel{3a}+8b & =-4 \\\\ 16b & =16 \\\\ b & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $b=1$ into the equation $-3a+8b=-4$ to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3a+8b & =-4 \\\\ -3a+8\\cdot 1 & =-4 \\\\ -3a+8 & =-4 \\\\ -3a & =-12 \\\\ a & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=4$ and $b=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bba6eef-b620-5c41-a7aa-66bcd70416e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252481} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7bf387-a643-5ae7-ac34-ee18d4d3667f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252482} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0f0fac-a601-5316-b6b7-9ece41f2fb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\,q=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252483} {:answer/id #uuid "6e169c4f-fa97-59ce-9197-f637dce1bc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=9,\\,q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252484} {:answer/id #uuid "7700ac3f-544e-5951-aa38-4a393d6ed87f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e7bf387-a643-5ae7-ac34-ee18d4d3667f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252482} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd7d036-c3b3-5aa0-af10-0828094c3b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252480}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92288a0c-f633-5c57-b73b-6161a16e5a13" :question/math-academy-id "q-216042" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3p+\\frac{4}{5}q=-1 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $5.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3p+\\frac{4}{5}q=-1 & |\\,\\times 5 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-15p+4q=-5 \\\\ 9p-4q=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $4q$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-4q$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $q$-variable, and allows us to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15p+\\cancel{4q} & =-5 \\\\ 9p-\\cancel{4q} & =-13 \\\\ -6p & =-18 \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $p=3$ into the equation $9p-4q=-13$ to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9p-4q & =-13 \\\\ 9\\cdot 3-4q & =-13 \\\\ 27-4q & =-13 \\\\ -4q & =-40 \\\\ q & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=3,\\,q=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cef8c113-0295-52ea-9e70-1c1a65fe5937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2,\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252488} {:answer/id #uuid "ccfc0f0f-f464-57d7-9c43-fbe4849602a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252489} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6e0ebb-4fbf-594e-8e5d-4d7663863709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3,\\,y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252490} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5bd586-fee3-5b90-a97c-a4ac034e7731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6,\\,y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252491} {:answer/id #uuid "d8dceb62-ce6f-57f8-aa91-be87b574b8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c5bd586-fee3-5b90-a97c-a4ac034e7731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6,\\,y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252491} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6751d96-4863-5fef-bf8c-4e8837e454e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252487}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7031c3ac-9f5c-549e-a734-1e40943152a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-216049" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5x-9y=-12 \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}x-2y=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5x-9y=-12 \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}x-2y=9 & |\\,\\times 6\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-5x-9y=-12 \\\\ 5x-12y=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $-5x$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $5x$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable, and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{5x}-9y & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}-12y & =54 \\\\ -21y & =42 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-2$ into the equation $-5x-9y=-12$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x-9y & =-12 \\\\ -5x-9\\cdot (-2) & =-12 \\\\ -5x+18 & =-12 \\\\ -5x & =-30 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=6,\\,y=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c707d476-d48d-5a31-83e0-d14ca6585762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252495} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8bda0f-5b8d-5b38-8bb8-9bddf729c0fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252496} {:answer/id #uuid "553c5845-1ba4-5783-8959-1201ea7a4579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252497} {:answer/id #uuid "ab70aeca-77c5-5204-ac3b-258559625507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2,\\,y=-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252498} {:answer/id #uuid "56da5624-b584-5144-b0cd-ad4780a04f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56da5624-b584-5144-b0cd-ad4780a04f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252499} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7fd1a2-4f2c-5595-93ee-d748093f560d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252494}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e810c346-0f80-58b2-8a40-15d2db43a85d" :question/math-academy-id "q-216059" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}y=-1 \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{7}y=\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}y=-1 & |\\times 3 \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{7}y=\\frac{1}{7} & |\\times 7\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4x+y=-3 \\\\ 7x+3y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the first equation can easily be solved for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+y & =-3 \\\\ y & =-3-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y=-3-4x$ into the second equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+3y & =1 \\\\ 7x+3(-3-4x) & =1 \\\\ 7x-9-12x & =1 \\\\ -5x & =10 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $y,$ we substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $y=-3-4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3-4x \\\\  & =-3-4(-2) \\\\  & =-3+8 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2$ and $y=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad329c1-6776-5fe7-8330-4c38a259ab06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252502} {:answer/id #uuid "4558fc9b-97c8-5cae-a42a-27d235f80e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ad329c1-6776-5fe7-8330-4c38a259ab06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252502} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c3e29aa-2b80-5f6a-a7a9-21580c2118ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252501} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dd76742-e5ea-5b04-9764-2bbf88e7df59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dd76742-e5ea-5b04-9764-2bbf88e7df59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252505} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b8c8a47-a4f2-5cef-8f1e-6918ac36e4a3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252504}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c1fa185-fc78-508c-827a-049d99108d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-216066" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{6}v-\\frac{1}{6}w=2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}v+\\frac{1}{5}w=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$v=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,w=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $6$ and the second equation by $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{6}v-\\frac{1}{6}w=2 & |\\times 6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}v+\\frac{1}{5}w=2 & |\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4v-w=12 \\\\ 2v+w=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+w & =10 \\\\ w & =10-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $w=10-2v$ into the first equation and then solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4v-w & =12 \\\\ 4v-(10-2v) & =12 \\\\ 4v-10+2v & =12 \\\\ 6v & =22 \\\\ v & =\\frac{22}{6} \\\\ v & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $w,$ we substitute $v=\\frac{11}{3}$ into the equation $w=10-2v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =10-2v\\text{ } \\\\  & =10-2(\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\  & =10-\\frac{22}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-22}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $v=\\frac{11}{3}$ and $w=\\frac{8}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e62da2e-fe3d-5b86-becc-1af1ace8a6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=5,\\,w=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252508} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1e6f27-62b4-5ba3-92b5-e1046ad28cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=-3,\\,w=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252509} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed5804a-1aae-54e2-90cf-8172a2c174fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=9,\\,w=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252510} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb91101-157d-5632-af99-cf5cc918710f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=7,\\,w=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252511} {:answer/id #uuid "cc217f3b-7277-5dba-8522-0fa9277adb2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=2,\\,w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdb91101-157d-5632-af99-cf5cc918710f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u=7,\\,w=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252511} :answer-field/id #uuid "05389c48-39b0-572f-a429-50c67dc31733" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252507}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27c1063e-f4a2-5692-98c2-9ec7c68b6839" :question/math-academy-id "q-216071" :question/problem "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}u-\\frac{1}{3}w=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}u-\\frac{1}{2}w=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $6$ and the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}u-\\frac{1}{3}w=\\frac{1}{2} & |\\times 6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}u-\\frac{1}{2}w=\\frac{2}{5} & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u-2w=3 \\\\ 2u-5w=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the first equation can easily be solved for $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u-2w & =3 \\\\ u & =2w+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $u=2w+3$ into the second equation and then solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u-5w & =4 \\\\ 2(2w+3)-5w & =4 \\\\ 4w+6-5w & =4 \\\\ -w+6 & =4 \\\\ -w & =-2 \\\\ w & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $u,$ we substitute $w=2$ into the equation $u=2w+3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2w+3 \\\\  & =2(2)+3 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $u=7$ and $w=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "216262a7-20f5-5120-b25a-369da1cc8db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "216262a7-20f5-5120-b25a-369da1cc8db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252515} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2c8284c-97cb-52d4-ad50-7cf979c81b30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5a3923f-a19d-5b89-a311-b6504e803f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5a3923f-a19d-5b89-a311-b6504e803f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252517} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fa289ca-997f-5f40-ab58-519a72ce3113" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92d72b3d-3f0b-503b-a282-400f532d2a50" :question/math-academy-id "q-216075" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5p+q=3 \\\\ 6p-\\frac{1}{4}q=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $4.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5p+q=3 \\\\ 6p-\\frac{1}{4}q=-8 & |\\,\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5p+q=3 \\\\ 24p-q=-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $q$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-q$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $q$-variable, and allows us to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5p+\\cancel{q} & =3 \\\\ 24p-\\cancel{q} & =-32 \\\\ 29p & =-29 \\\\ p & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $p=-1$ into the equation $5p+q=3$ to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5p+q & =3 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-1)+q & =3 \\\\ -5+q & =3 \\\\ q & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-1,\\,q=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d58c9f6-d6c8-534d-bff5-8be1c9bdd492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d58c9f6-d6c8-534d-bff5-8be1c9bdd492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232403} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7987735-a6e2-518c-a1de-a5ef26b254e7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12ca6bca-2d8a-5010-8df2-0c06586035f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12ca6bca-2d8a-5010-8df2-0c06586035f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232402} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29b023e-063c-57f9-9180-b6fdb001845c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095057}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ad0b8d4-1ab9-5165-9ffa-778e033d3dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-216178" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${-\\frac{5}{2}a + 3b =-6; \\frac{5}{3}a - \\frac{1}{3}b = 9$\n\n$a =$\n{{field-1}} $, b =$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}a+3b=-6 & |\\times 2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}a-\\frac{1}{3}b=9 & |\\times 3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-5a+6b=-12 \\\\ 5a-b=27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $a$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{5a}+6b & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{5a}-b & =27 \\\\ (6b-b) & =(-12+27) \\\\ 5b & =15 \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $b=3$ into the second equation to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-b & =27 \\\\ 5a-3 & =27 \\\\ 5a & =30 \\\\ a & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $a=6,\\,b=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad0370a2-f291-5c05-8fcb-55081034cdae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad0370a2-f291-5c05-8fcb-55081034cdae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252520} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9d7af9-b3e4-533e-a7e9-3b85602e7f17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252519} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5ac7636-c5bd-58a8-a375-1daa5b3c2995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5ac7636-c5bd-58a8-a375-1daa5b3c2995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252522} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcca4943-105b-5ffd-83e6-1d85660f411c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252521}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcdf7481-e9da-5b94-b4eb-a65b1ffb01f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-216199" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a-\\frac{5}{4}b=30 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}a+b=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a-\\frac{5}{4}b=30 & |\\times 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}a+b=-6 & |\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}8a-5b=120 \\\\ a+5b=-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $b$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8a-\\cancel{5b} & =120 \\\\ a+\\cancel{5b} & =-30 \\\\ (8a+a) & =(120-30) \\\\ 9a & =90 \\\\ a & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $a=10$ into the second equation to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+5b & =-30 \\\\ 10+5b & =-30 \\\\ 5b & =-40 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ b & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $a=10,\\,b=-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d010d9d-f4ee-591e-9d8d-1ab1bf3029da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=6,\\,b=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252525} {:answer/id #uuid "13a2a89b-0eb5-5afa-b7e8-35a5ffc87a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-8,\\,b=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252526} {:answer/id #uuid "07480345-aec5-5c4e-a528-0c35344d7771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=8,\\,b=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252527} {:answer/id #uuid "14fa8223-3e30-57d8-abd9-a52e0c4a7a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-4,\\,b=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252528} {:answer/id #uuid "9179a10a-62ff-52f6-b1c2-6f6d01375428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-4,\\,b=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a2a89b-0eb5-5afa-b7e8-35a5ffc87a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-8,\\,b=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252526} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0b6550-4b96-5988-ba37-4ab11834de74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252524}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f83cc86-a896-545c-8edd-a31db10e015a" :question/math-academy-id "q-216309" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}a+b=-3 \\\\ a-\\frac{1}{3}b=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}a+b=-3 & |\\times 4 \\\\ a-\\frac{1}{3}b=-9 & |\\times 3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3a+4b=-12 \\\\ 3a-b=-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $-3a$ term that can cancel with the $3a$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3a+4b=-12 & |\\,\\times (-1) \\\\ 3a-b=-27 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3a-4b=12 \\\\ 3a-b=-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $a$-variable, and allows us to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{3a}-4b & =12 \\\\ \\cancel{3a}-b & =-27 \\\\ (-4b-b) & =(12-27) \\\\ -5b & =-15 \\\\ b & =\\frac{-15}{-5} \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $b=3$ into the second equation to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-b & =-27 \\\\ 3a-3 & =-27 \\\\ 3a & =-24 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{24}{3} \\\\ a & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $a=-8,\\,b=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e63e066-2f8b-5d34-ac78-0c2587880309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252532} {:answer/id #uuid "89fea6f1-2c25-5086-84ab-0beebc533fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2,\\,y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252533} {:answer/id #uuid "a7594cdc-5fca-519d-baa8-7cd8f381d612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252534} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3de9f5-1e0e-5678-a1a9-6ad59daf10f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252535} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8cc5a2-98d3-552e-830d-ea43611ee8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3,\\,y=-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7594cdc-5fca-519d-baa8-7cd8f381d612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252534} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fcf1598-e93b-50fb-9d77-1ca3089bc11c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252531}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a438562b-561a-5a07-8688-ac5551c46746" :question/math-academy-id "q-26094" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{5y}{6}-2=0 \\\\ 3x+y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{5y}{6}-2=0 & |\\,\\times 6 \\\\ 3x+y+6=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x+5y-12=0 \\\\ 3x+y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y=-3x-6$ into the first equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5y-12 & =0 \\\\ x+5(-3x-6)-12 & =0 \\\\ x-15x-30-12 & =0 \\\\ -14x-42 & =0 \\\\ -14x & =42 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $y,$ we substitute $x=-3$ into the equation $y=-3x-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-6 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-6 \\\\  & =9-6 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6cf477-5ce2-54f4-959c-7a8b0448196c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=5,\\,w=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252539} {:answer/id #uuid "de0c61b5-0807-5f50-8201-7b875f7b4a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=1,\\,w=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252540} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c685e2-4e75-535f-8f2d-5cfd3706d5f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=\\frac{1}{2},\\,w=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252541} {:answer/id #uuid "7644c12c-ffc7-5139-aadf-8a50bca98fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=6,\\,w=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252542} {:answer/id #uuid "1df6e996-fa00-5ebd-8182-593f4d0645d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=5,\\,w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1df6e996-fa00-5ebd-8182-593f4d0645d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=5,\\,w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252543} :answer-field/id #uuid "60880ebc-476c-595d-ad75-495f4c1eaea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e136b8d-f97f-56d2-a8c3-ae7a8f77031b" :question/math-academy-id "q-52200" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2v+3w=10 \\\\ v-\\frac{3}{4}w=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $4.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2v+3w=10 \\\\ v-\\frac{3}{4}w=5 & |\\,\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2v+3w=10 \\\\ 4v-3w=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $3w$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-3w$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $w$-variable, and allows us to solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+\\cancel{3w} & =10 \\\\ 4v-\\cancel{3w} & =20 \\\\ 6v & =30 \\\\ v & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $v=5$ into the equation $4v-3w=20$ to solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4v-3w & =20 \\\\ 4\\cdot 5-3w & =20 \\\\ 20-3w & =20 \\\\ -3w & =0 \\\\ w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $v=5$ and $w=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f639cd4-b5c0-5f11-9565-51a95891e8a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3,\\,w=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252546} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdca147-cf66-5649-a213-77c1ddb68193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4,\\,w=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252547} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8f5e21-9228-5b72-b044-dc8b0ac3b08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=3,\\,w=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252548} {:answer/id #uuid "541d2208-700b-55e9-9792-2e7d10cc9bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3,\\,w=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252549} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6a5dd7-f9cc-5799-9258-14f47a54b6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=5,\\,w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "541d2208-700b-55e9-9792-2e7d10cc9bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3,\\,w=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252549} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd628f1a-2548-51f4-869f-7589a6eda7be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252545}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1145ce5a-6b11-53ed-9fcc-76e83be7ade1" :question/math-academy-id "q-52202" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}v-2w=5 \\\\ -v-\\frac{2}{5}w=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}v-2w=5 & |\\times 3 \\\\ -v-\\frac{2}{5}w=5 & |\\times 5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5v-6w=15 \\\\ -5v-2w=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $v$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{5v}-6w & =15 \\\\ -\\cancel{5v}-2w & =25 \\\\ (-6w-2w) & =(15+25) \\\\ -8w & =40 \\\\ w & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $w=-5$ into the first equation to solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5v-6w & =15 \\\\ 5v-6(-5) & =15 \\\\ 5v+30 & =15 \\\\ 5v & =-15 \\\\ v & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $v=-3,\\,w=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186252551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50b03ae3-1445-5c9c-9e58-b705b006b72d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252553} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3afc8e-03c7-57ee-8f67-a0ffee7c4270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,y=-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252554} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b2acee-6697-5d93-8d8d-13565f1017c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252555} {:answer/id #uuid "17b4c048-4ff1-5569-a7bf-acfa129e2da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252556} {:answer/id #uuid "d633e03f-6c9f-5d7e-b7b0-d5a739ca76b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17b4c048-4ff1-5569-a7bf-acfa129e2da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252556} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eab116e-7fd3-5f6d-9c61-4b026cc745b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252552}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cd25c8c-eb40-551c-be66-2948be61be80" :question/math-academy-id "q-75862" :question/problem "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 & |\\times 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1 & |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ 2x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $y$ term that can cancel with the $-y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 &  \\\\ 2x-y=4 & |\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ -2x+y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\cancel{y} & =6 \\\\ -2x+\\cancel{y} & =-4 \\\\ (6x-2x) & =(6-4) \\\\ 4x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equation to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =4 \\\\ 2(\\frac{1}{2})-y & =4 \\\\ 1-y & =4 \\\\ y & =1-4 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3.$"}]}
